WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.263
Organizations cannot defend what they cannot observe.

2
00:00:03.263 --> 00:00:16.872
Telemetry provides visibility into activity across the environment and gives security teams evidence they can use to recognize suspicious behavior, investigate incidents, and understand whether safeguards are working as intended.

3
00:00:16.872 --> 00:00:19.796
Visibility improves operational decisions.

4
00:00:19.796 --> 00:00:33.176
Analysts can compare current activity with expected patterns, correlate events from multiple systems, establish timelines, and determine which users, devices, applications, or resources may be involved.

5
00:00:33.176 --> 00:00:37.107
Without reliable data, teams are forced to rely on assumptions.

6
00:00:37.107 --> 00:00:41.128
Telemetry also helps organizations measure security effectiveness.

7
00:00:41.128 --> 00:00:54.029
Teams can evaluate whether important systems are reporting, whether detections receive the data they require, how quickly incidents are discovered, and where blind spots or retention limits could weaken an investigation.

8
00:00:54.029 --> 00:00:56.274
More data is not automatically better.

9
00:00:56.274 --> 00:01:01.682
Effective programs collect telemetry for clear security and operational purposes.

10
00:01:01.682 --> 00:01:12.279
They balance visibility with cost, privacy, retention, system performance, and analyst workload so that collection produces useful evidence rather than unmanaged volume.

11
00:01:12.279 --> 00:01:17.657
A practical visibility review starts with important services and plausible security questions.

12
00:01:17.657 --> 00:01:26.257
Teams ask whether they could identify who accessed a resource, what changed, which systems communicated, and how far back they could investigate.

13
00:01:26.257 --> 00:01:34.379
Answers expose blind spots more clearly than a simple count of connected log sources and help leaders prioritize improvement work.
